$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{5-\sqrt{3x+28}}{x+1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5-\sqrt{3x+28}}{x+1}\cdot\dfrac{5+\sqrt{3x+28}}{5+\sqrt{3x+28}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the numerator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{5^2-(3x+28)}{(x+1)(5+\sqrt{3x+28})}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-3\cancel{(x+1)}}{\cancel{(x+1)}(5+\sqrt{3x+28})}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{-3}{5+\sqrt{3x+28}} \text{, for }x\neq -1
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $-1$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
